/tmp/tmp.DjDellTccI/main.tex:770:While we have shown that absorbers exist, we still have to justify why we can indeed ``find'' them inside a given initial $k$-configuration. By ``finding'', we mean that we can choose a few red edges such that the desired absorber appears inside the reduced configuration. 
/tmp/tmp.DjDellTccI/main.tex:806:Next, we apply the previous lemma several times in order to find the desired absorber.
/tmp/tmp.DjDellTccI/main.tex:819:    (Such a set $Z$ exists as $\cM$ is the union of $k$ perfect matchings and has at least $2C$ vertices.)
/tmp/tmp.DjDellTccI/main.tex:845:We can now show the main result of this section: Configurations which, for each colour, contain enough edges of that colour only, admit a red perfect matching.
/tmp/tmp.DjDellTccI/main.tex:849:For every $j \in [s]$, by Lemma~\ref{lem:abs}, there exists a $k$-configuration $\cA_j$ such that $\cA_j$ is an absorber for $\cL_j$. We may assume that the $\cA_j$'s have pairwise disjoint vertex sets, and we let $\cA^*:=\cA_1\cup \dots \cup \cA_s$.
/tmp/tmp.DjDellTccI/main.tex:852:Note that, since $\cA^*$ is a configuration, $\cM_1$ has no edges between $U$ and $W:=V(\cM_1)\sm U$. In $\cM_1[W]$, we run the greedy algorithm to find a partial red matching $M^*_2$ that covers all but $m$ vertices. Let $\cL$ be the reduced configuration on these $m$ vertices. Then it must be isomorphic to $\cL_{j_0}$ for some $j_0\in [s]$. Now, for each $j\neq j_0$, there is a totally equalizing red matching for $\cA_j$, and there is a totally equalizing red matching for $\cL_{j_0}\cup \cA_{j_0}$ (and thus for $\cL \cup A_{j_0}$). Let $M^*_3$ be the union of all these red matchings. 
/tmp/tmp.DjDellTccI/main.tex:853:Hence, $M^*_1\cup M^*_2 \cup M^*_3$ is a totally equalizing matching of $\cM$, and we can easily choose the remaining red edges to find a red perfect matching for~$\cM$.
